          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Virginity symbolizes purity, chastity, and exclusive covenant fidelity, especially Israel or the Church as the faithful bride pledged to God or Christ.\",\"book_summary\":\"A virgin signifies someone covenantally espoused to one husband while the wedding remains future; the emphasis is betrothal status rather than achieved purity, with harlotry as the opposite.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Purity and exclusive covenant faithfulness embodied in the virgin-bride or faithful people of God.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The covenant status of being betrothed to one husband before the wedding.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts the symbol to pre-wedding espousal and reclassifies passages about virgin Israel, the Church, and wedding attendants around covenantal betrothal. It excludes or sidelines broader consensus uses centered on literal sexual virginity, general chastity, ascetic consecration, vulnerability, or readiness apart from espousal.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction at the central referent: covenant betrothal is already prominent in the consensus. The material difference is that the book makes relational standing—not purity or attained moral condition—the defining feature.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings locate the principal symbolism in the marriage-covenant relationship between God's people and one divine husband. The book therefore does not replace the consensus referent, but it materially sharpens and narrows it: 'virgin' means the espoused party awaiting marriage, including restored Israel, rather than purity or fidelity in the broader sense. That boundary change makes the relationship REFINED rather than MATCH or DIVERGENT.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Several citations establish a wedding or betrothal setting, especially Deuteronomy 22:23–24 and 2 Corinthians 11:2, but they do not prove that espousal is the term's defining meaning in every cited case. Psalm 45's virgins are the bride's companions, Matthew 25's virgins may be wedding attendants, and Jeremiah and Amos call Israel a virgin without the excerpts themselves demonstrating re-espousal after divorce. The claim that covenant standing rather than purity is decisive therefore requires argument beyond the printed citations.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
